/*Q1. Write a Java program to demonstrate multithreading by generating random numbers and performing arithmetic operations based on their
parity.
Create the following thread classes:

• RandomNumberThread

• SquareThread

• CubeThread

Requirements:

1. Create a class named RandomNumberThread that extends Thread.

2. The RandomNumberThread must generate exactly three random integers using a Random object initialized with seed value 50.
3. Generate each random integer in the range 0 to 99 inclusive using random.nextInt(100).

4. For each generated integer:

• Display the generated integer.

• If the number is even, create and start a SquareThread to calculate and display its square.

• If the number is odd, create and start a CubeThread to calculate and display its cube.

5. Introduce a delay of 1000 milliseconds between successive random number generations using Thread.sleep(1000).
6. Create a class named SquareThread that extends Thread. Its constructor must accept an integer, and its run() method must display the
square of that integer.
7. Create a class named CubeThread that extends Thread. Its constructor must accept an integer, and its run() method must display the cube
of that integer.
8. In the main() method of the public class ClassMthread, create and start a RandomNumberThread .
Input Format:
• No input is required.
Output Format:
For each generated random integer, first display:
Random Integer generated : number
Then display the corresponding arithmetic result:
• For an even number:
Square of number = square
• For an odd number:
Cube of number = cube
Repeat this process for all three generated integers.
Example Output:
Random Integer generated : 42
Square of 42 = 1764
Random Integer generated : 29
Cube of 29 = 24389
Random Integer generated : 74
Square of 74 = 5476
Constraints:
• Generate exactly three random integers.
• Use seed value 50.
• Each random integer must be in the range 0 to 99, inclusive.
• Use Thread.sleep(1000) to introduce the required delay between successive generations.
• Match the specified output text, capitalization, punctuation, and spacing.
Note:
• Refer to the visible test cases to strictly match the input/output layout.
*/
//////////////////////////
import java.util.Random;
 
class SquareThread extends Thread {
    int number;
 
    SquareThread(int number) {
        this.number = number;
    }
 
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Square of " + number + " = " + (number * number));
    }
}
 
class CubeThread extends Thread {
    int number;
 
    CubeThread(int number) {
        this.number = number;
    }
 
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Cube of " + number + " = " + (number * number * number));
    }
}
 
class RandomNumberThread extends Thread {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        Random random = new Random(50);
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            int num = random.nextInt(100);
            System.out.println("Random Integer generated : " + num);
            Thread t = (num % 2 == 0) ? new SquareThread(num) : new CubeThread(num);
            t.start();
            try {
                t.join();                 // keep the result right after its number
                Thread.sleep(1000);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                System.out.println(e);
            }
        }
    }
}
 
public class ClassMthread {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RandomNumberThread r = new RandomNumberThread();
        r.start();
    }
}
